    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d803">

                <pb facs="0968" n="878" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg878"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72113/1392</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Honduran Minister</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">López Gutiérrez</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-30"><hi rendition="#italic">December 30,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of December 27,<anchor n="26" xml:id="d803-f1"/> advising me that
                    you have received a telegram from your Government stating that the Treaty of
                    Peace with Germany will probably be approved when it is presented to the
                    Congress of <pb facs="0969" n="879" xml:id="pg879"/> Honduras in January next,
                    for ratification, and that all matters relating to German residents of Honduras
                    will be taken up for discussion at the same time.</p>
                <p>In this general connection you state that your Government purposes to introduce
                    in the Congress of Honduras a bill establishing a monopoly in favor of the State
                    of the business of agencies in the port of Amapala, the Government endeavoring
                    to grant fair compensation to the parties interested for properties taken over
                    from them. You express the hope that in view of the desire of your Government to
                    act in accord with the Government of the United States, this solution of the
                    question will prove acceptable to the Department.</p>
                <p>In reply, I have the honor to inform you that the Department of State considers
                    that the matter is one to be determined by the Government of Honduras for itself
                    and trusts that a solution may be arrived at which will be satisfactory to the
                    Government of Honduras. The Department of State of course understands that the
                    Government of Honduras would not be satisfied with a solution which would not be
                    just to all parties concerned.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Norman H. Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
